Koda runs in two modes:

  INTERACTIVE   Run `koda` (no arguments) to open the full TUI.
                Type your question and press Enter.
                Type /help inside for keybindings and all commands.

  EXEC          `koda exec` runs one prompt without the TUI and exits.
                For scripts, pipes and CI pipelines.
                  koda exec "explain this codebase"
                  git diff | koda exec
                  koda exec --json "fix the failing tests"

Configuration precedence (highest wins):
  1. CLI flags     --model, --provider, --base-url
  2. Env vars      KODA_MODEL, KODA_PROVIDER, KODA_BASE_URL
  3. Saved config  set interactively with /model, /provider, /key
                   (a --base-url used in the TUI is remembered too)
  4. Built-in defaults

API keys (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY, …)
follow the same order. Keys saved with /key are loaded from the
local keystore at startup and injected as env vars — shell env
vars always win over stored keys.

Usage: koda [OPTIONS] [PROMPT] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  exec  Run one prompt without the TUI, then exit
  mcp   Manage the MCP servers every session starts: list, get, add, remove
  help  Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  [PROMPT]
          Open the TUI and send this prompt, as `codex "prompt"` does. A one-shot run is `koda exec "prompt"`

Options:
  -a, --agent <AGENT>
          Agent to use (matches a JSON file in agents/)
          
          [default: default]

  -s, --resume <SESSION>
          Session ID to resume (omit to start a new session)

      --project-root <PROJECT_ROOT>
          Project root directory (defaults to current directory)

      --base-url <BASE_URL>
          LLM provider base URL override
          
          [env: KODA_BASE_URL=]

      --model <MODEL>
          Model name override
          
          [env: KODA_MODEL=]

      --provider <PROVIDER>
          LLM provider (openai, anthropic, lmstudio, llama-cpp, gemini, groq, grok, ollama)
          
          [env: KODA_PROVIDER=]

      --max-tokens <MAX_TOKENS>
          Maximum output tokens

      --max-context-tokens <TOKENS>
          Context window in tokens, for servers that don't report theirs. Beats the agent config, the server's answer and the model table
          
          [env: KODA_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=]

      --temperature <TEMPERATURE>
          Sampling temperature (0.0 - 2.0)

      --thinking-budget <THINKING_BUDGET>
          Gemini thinking budget in tokens. Claude models take --effort instead

      --reasoning-effort <LEVEL>
          Reasoning effort. Claude: low, medium, high, xhigh or max; by default the level Claude Code uses for the model. OpenAI-compatible servers: passed through as `reasoning_effort`
          
          [env: KODA_REASONING_EFFORT=]

      --mode <MODE>
          Trust mode: auto (the default, unless config.toml sets `mode`), safe, or plan (`koda exec` only). "auto" auto-approves mutating actions; the kernel sandbox contains them. Destructive ops (rm -rf, git reset --hard, git push --force) and outside-project writes still confirm. Auto requires the kernel sandbox; on unsandboxed platforms koda refuses to start with an actionable error (use --mode safe or install the platform sandbox backend). "safe" confirms every mutation before executing — use this in CI, locked-down workstations, or any context where you want a human in every approval loop. "plan" is read-only, for a `koda exec` run that should only look: reads, searches, read-only commands and MCP tools marked read-only run; everything else is refused, and the kernel sandbox keeps the project read-only. Sandbox with credential protection is always active
          
          [env: KODA_MODE=]
          [possible values: safe, auto, plan]

  -c, --config <KEY=VALUE>
          Override a config.toml value for this run: `-c key=value`, with a dotted key (`-c model_providers.lm-studio.base_url=http://...`). The value is read as TOML, else taken as text, as Codex's `-c` is. Repeatable; never saved. Flags such as --model win over it

      --mcp-config <FILE|JSON>
          Add MCP servers for this run only, from a JSON file or inline JSON in the `{"mcpServers": {...}}` shape of Claude Code's `.mcp.json`. Repeatable; later servers win a name clash, and all of them win over servers stored with `koda mcp add`. Nothing is saved

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Examples:
  koda                                     # interactive TUI (type /help inside)
  koda "fix the failing test"              # the TUI, starting with that prompt
  koda -s abc123                           # resume a saved session
  koda exec "explain this codebase"       # one-shot question, then exit
  koda exec - < prompt.txt                 # read the prompt from stdin
  git diff | koda exec                     # pipe a diff as the prompt
  koda exec --json "fix the failing tests" # the client protocol on stdout
  koda exec --model o3 "refactor"         # one-shot with a specific model
  koda exec -s abc123 "continue"          # continue a saved session
